-- title:
--   Quotient scalars on an ordinary line satisfy z²≡ 1
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative local ring and let $\rho$ be a two-dimensional adic Galois representation over $A$ in the sense of [`GaloisRepAdic`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16): a finite free $A$-module $\rho.V$ of rank $2$ together with a monoid homomorphism $\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to $\mathrm{End}_A(\rho.V)$ that is continuous for the $\mathfrak m_A$-adic filtration. Let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$. Assume `DetIsCyclotomic`: $p \in \mathfrak m_A$, and for all $n$, all $\sigma$ and all $a \in \mathbb N$, if $\sigma\mu = \mu^a$ for every $p^n$-th root of unity $\mu$, then $\det \rho(\sigma) - a$ lies in the ideal generated by $p^n$. Assume further that the representation over the residue field $A/\mathfrak m_A$ obtained from $\rho$ by reduction, viewed as an adic representation, is strictly ordinary at $p$: the residue characteristic condition holds, and for every valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $p$ is a non-unit there is a line spanned by the first vector of a basis which is stable under the decomposition group, on whose quotient inertia acts trivially, and on which each decomposition-group element $\sigma$ acts by a scalar $x$ with quotient scalar $z$ satisfying $x - a z \in (p^n)$ whenever $\sigma$ raises $p^n$-th roots of unity to the $a$-th power. Let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ a non-unit in $P$, let $L \subseteq \rho.V$ be a submodule of the form $A \cdot b_0$ for some basis $b$ of $\rho.V$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, and assume that every element of the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $P$ acts trivially on $\rho.V/L$, i.e. $\rho(\tau)v - v \in L$ for all $v$. Let $\sigma$ lie in the decomposition subgroup of $P$ and let $z \in A$ satisfy $\rho(\sigma)v - z v \in L$ for all $v \in \rho.V$. Then $z^2 - 1 \in \mathfrak m_A$. The line $L$ is not assumed to be stable under the decomposition group.
--
--   This is the residual input to the passage from the ordinary to the strictly ordinary local condition at $p$ (Wiles, Chapter 1 §1; Darmon–Diamond–Taylor §2.4): modulo $\mathfrak m_A$ the scalar by which $\sigma$ acts on the quotient by an inertia-trivial line is forced to be a square root of unity, which pins down the relation between the two characters on the line and its quotient. It is used in the proof that a representation satisfying the ordinary condition whose residual representation is très ramifiée satisfies the strict ordinary condition.
